The latter is patently false.

To address the former, scrolls (that aren't in your starting
inventory) are labelled, DUAM XNAHT, ELBIB YLOH, XIXAXA XOXAXA XUXAXA,
etc.  Once you have a) read them to know what they do, or b)
identified them by another means, then they show up as a scroll of
identify, scroll of enchant weapon, etc.

Scrolls of identify are the cheapest scrolls in the game, typically
costing between 15 and 39 $, depending mainly on your CHR.  None are
ever guaranteed, but they are among the more common scrolls (based on
chance of being generated, which of course is totally random).

Also, if you have unidentified scrolls, and drop them in a shop that
sells scrolls, you should be offered money to sell it.  If you are
offered a very small amount (say, 8-15 $), tell him no thanks, and
pick up your scroll of identify.

If you have reason to believe a scroll is, in fact, identify, yet
don't want to read it, name the object.  Command n, answer n to the
query, then select the inventory letter of your scroll.  Call a scroll
labeled THARR: identify

Then, you no longer have a scroll labeled THARR.  You have a scroll
called identify.  Of course, if it's not REALLY a scroll of identify,
this does no good... it's just a way for you to remember what (you
think) the scroll is.

